[1]A bakery prepares n items every morning and places them in a display row. At the end of each hour, the leftmost item is moved to the rightmost position to 
make room for fresh stock at the front. Given the initial row and the number of hours h, print the final display order.Describe the approach you used. How does 
it behave when h is very large, say 10 million? Is there a way to get the correct result without performing the operation h times?
Problem faced :
-->At first, I did not understand how the items move after each hour.
-->I was confused about which item should move first.
-->It was difficult to keep the items in the correct order after every rotation.
-->I first thought I had to repeat the process n times.
-->I did not know that the same pattern repeats after some rotations.
-->I had to think carefully about what happens when the number of hours is very large.

[2]A library issues books to students and records each book's ID every time it is borrowed. At the end of the month, the librarian wants to find all books that
